# Holes

Setting: `ext_holes`.

A hole is an expression in a document, for a consumer to evaluate: the
document is a template and the hole is where a value goes, as in an
article template literal (Crichton and Krishnamurthi, "A Core Calculus
for Documents", POPL 2024). The parser records the expression as written
and never reads it. What language it is in, how it is evaluated, and
whether its value is a node, a list or an option are the consumer's.

``` example
Today I am going shopping for %`List.length items` things.
.
<p>Today I am going shopping for <code data-hole="">List.length items</code> things.</p>
```

The HTML renderer shows a hole nobody evaluated as its expression, so it
is not lost silently.

## A hole is a code span

A `%` directly before a code span makes it a hole, as a `$` makes it
math. Everything djot says of the code span holds of the hole: its
content is read verbatim, backslashes and braces included, a longer
backtick run lets it hold backticks, and a single space is removed on
each side when it starts or ends with a backtick.

``` example
%`{r with x = 1}` and %``f `A`` and %`print_string "\n"`
.
<p><code data-hole="">{r with x = 1}</code> and <code data-hole="">f `A</code> and <code data-hole="">print_string "\n"</code></p>
```

A hole may cross a line break, which is part of the expression, and one
the paragraph ends inside runs to its end, as a code span does.

``` example
a %`f
x` b %`g y
.
<p>a <code data-hole="">f
x</code> b <code data-hole="">g y</code></p>
```

An empty expression is still a hole, for the consumer to reject.

``` example
%``
.
<p><code data-hole=""></code></p>
```

Attributes after a hole are the hole's, as they are a code span's. A raw
format is not: as after math, `{=html}` is text.

``` example
%`x`{.c} and %`y`{=html}
.
<p><code data-hole="" class="c">x</code> and <code data-hole="">y</code>{=html}</p>
```

## What is not a hole

Only a `%` immediately before the backticks prefixes them, and `\%`
writes one that does not.

``` example
a % `x` and \%`y` and %{.c}
.
<p>a % <code>x</code> and %<code>y</code> and <span class="c">%</span></p>
```

A `%` inside a code span, an autolink or a link destination is not a
prefix.

``` example
``%`x`` and [a](%`u`)
.
<p><code>%`x</code> and <a href="%`u`">a</a></p>
```

## Priority

A hole opens at its backticks and closes at the matching run, as a code
span does, so whatever it holds is not markup and what opens first wins.

``` example
*a %`b* c` and `d %`e` f`
.
<p>*a <code data-hole="">b* c</code> and <code>d %</code>e<code> f</code></p>
```

## Text

A hole's text, from which a heading's identifier and an empty
reference's label are made, is its expression, as math's is its formula.

``` example
# Total %`n`
.
<section id="Total-n">
<h1>Total <code data-hole="">n</code></h1>
</section>
```

## Blocks

There is no block syntax. A raw block in format `hole` is a raw block to
the parser, kept exactly, blank lines and indentation included, and a
consumer that evaluates holes may read it as one. Like any raw block in
a format the HTML renderer does not know, it renders as nothing.

```` example
Items:

``` =hole
ul (List.map item items)
```
.
<p>Items:</p>
````

